\label{thm:main_theorem}
    There exists a smooth, mean convex embedding \(M_0\cong \mathbb{S}^{p+q-1}\) in \(\mathbb{R}^{p+q}\) whose evolution under the mean curvature flow has the following properties:
    \begin{enumerate}[(1)]
        \item the flow forms an isolated \(\mathbb{R}^p\times\mathbb{S}^{q-1}\) bubble-sheet singularity at the first singular time \(t_0\);
        \item there exists some \(\varepsilon>0\) such that each time slice \(M_{t_0+t}\) is a smooth embedding of \(\mathbb{S}^{q}\times \mathbb{S}^{p-1}\) for \(0<t < \varepsilon\).
    \end{enumerate}

\label{thm:sturmian_theorem}
    Let \(f = f(x,t,u,p,q)\) be a smooth function of its five variables, and let \(u\), \(v\) be two smooth solutions of the non-linear equation
    \begin{align}\label{eq:sturmian}
        \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = f(x,t,u,u_x,u_{xx}).
    \end{align}
    on \([x_1,x_2]\times [0,t_0]\). Assuming that \eqref{eq:sturmian} is parabolic, if \(u(x_j,t)\neq v(x_j,t)\) for \(0\leq t \leq t_0\), and \(j=1,2\), the number of zeros of \(x\mapsto v(x,t)-u(x,t)\), when counted with multiplicity\footnote{the number of zeros of \(f\) in \((a,b)\) counted with multiplicity is the sum of the orders of all zeros of \(f\) in \((a,b)\), where the order of a zero at \(x_0\) is the smallest integer \(k\) for which \(f^{(k)}(x_0)\neq 0\)}, is finite for any \(t\in (0,t_0]\), and does not increase with time. Moreover, at any time \(t\) where the zero is of order \(k>1\), the number of zeros at time \(t\) drops by at least \(k-1\).

\label{lem:disc_containment}
    Suppose \(M_0 = \Sigma_u\) satisfies the assumptions of Proposition \ref{prop:bubblesheet}, and let \(x_0 = (x^*,0,y^*,0)\in P_0\) be a point reached by the level set flow at the first singular time \(t_0\). Define
    \begin{align*}
        \ell &= \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in P_1:x_1 = 0\}, \
        &H_2^- &= \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in P_2: y_1 \leq 0\}, \\
        H_1^- &= \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in P_1:x_1\leq 0\}, \
        &H_1^+ &= \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in P_1:x_1\geq 0\}.
    \end{align*}
    The following statements hold:
    \begin{enumerate}[(a)]
        \item  if \(x^* > 0\) and \(y^*\geq 0\), then the tangent flow at \(X_0\) contains \(\ell\) in its interior;
        \item if \(y^*> 0\) and \(x^*\geq 0\), then the tangent flow at \(X_0\) contains \(H_2^-\) in its interior;
        \item if \(x^* >  0\) and \(y^* = 0\), then
        \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
            \item if \(M_0\) crosses \(P_2\), or if \(M_0\) avoids \(P_2\) and \(x_0\) is not the left \(P_1\)-crossing point of \(M_{t_0}\), then the tangent flow at \(X_0\) contains \(H_1^-\) in its interior;
            \item if \(M_0\) avoids \(P_2\) and \(x_0\) is the left \(P_1\)-crossing point of \(M_{t_0}\), then the tangent flow at \(X_0\) contains \(H_1^+\) in its interior.
        \end{enumerate}
    \end{enumerate}

Recall that at a singular point \(X_0=(x_0,t_0)\) of mean convex mean curvature flow, any sequence of rescalings \(\tilde{M}_t^{k} = s_k(M_{s_k^{-2}t + t_0}-x_0)\) converges, up to a subsequence, smoothly on any compact sub-interval of \((-\infty,0)\) to a shrinking sphere or cylinder (c.f. Section \ref{sec:tangent_flows}). In Proposition \ref{prop:bubblesheet}, we established that for \(M_0\) satisfying the conditions of Proposition \ref{prop:bubblesheet}, the tangent flow at a singularity can only be a shrinking \(\mathbb{R}^2\times\mathbb{S}^1\), with the \(\mathbb{S}^1\) factor parallel to \(P_2\). Since this is true for any sequence of rescalings, we obtain that for every fixed \(t'<0\), 
    \begin{align*}
        \lambda (M_{t_0 + \lambda^{-2}t'} - x_0) \to \sqrt{-t'} \left(\mathbb{R}^2\times \mathbb{S}^1_{\sqrt{2}}\right),
    \end{align*}
    smoothly as \(\lambda \to \infty\). Choosing \(\lambda = \left(\frac{-t'}{t_0-t}\right)^{1/2}\) (so \(\lambda\to \infty\) as \(t\to t_0\)) it follows that
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{1}{\sqrt{t_0-t}}(M_t - x_0)\to \mathbb{R}^2 \times \mathbb{S}^1_{\sqrt{2}}
    \end{align*}
    smoothly as \(t\to t_0\). Moreover, since \(P_0\) intersects \(\mathbb{R}^2 \times \mathbb{S}^1_{\sqrt{2}}\) transversely, the local smooth convergence still holds when both sides are intersected with \(P_0^+\coloneqq P_0 \cap \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in \mathbb{R}^4 :x_1\geq 0, \ x_3\geq 0\}\). We can write the convergence in terms of \(u\) as
    \begin{align*}
        \left\{\frac{1}{\sqrt{t_0-t}}((x,0,u(x,t),0) - (\xi,0,0,0)):x\in (\xi_1(t_0),\xi_2(t_0))\right\} 
        \to \left\{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in P_0^+: \ y_1 \equiv \sqrt{2}\right\}.
    \end{align*}
    Performing the change of variables \(z = (x-\xi)/\sqrt{t_0-t}\), it follows that 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:blowup_limit}
        \lim_{t\to t_0} \frac{u(\xi + z\sqrt{t_0-t},t)}{\sqrt{t_0-t}} = \sqrt{2}
    \end{align}
    uniformly in \(z\) on any bounded interval \(|z|\leq M\).

    Now fix \(\varepsilon>0\) and let \(M = \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{\varepsilon}\). There exists \(t_{\varepsilon}<t_0\) such that 
    \begin{align*}
        u(\xi + z\sqrt{t_0-t},t) \leq 2\sqrt{2(t_0-t)}
    \end{align*}
    for \(|z|\leq M\), and \(t_\varepsilon < t < t_0\). Let \(\mu_\varepsilon = M \sqrt{t_0-t}\), then any \(x\neq \xi\) with \(|x - \xi|\leq \mu_\varepsilon\) can be written as \(x = \xi\pm M\sqrt{t_0-t}\) for \(t_\varepsilon < t< t_0\). The mean convexity of the flow implies that \(u(\cdot,t)\) moves strictly downwards with time, and so we find that for such \(x\),
    \begin{equation*}
        u(x,t_0) 
        < u(x,t) 
        \leq 2\sqrt{2(t_0-t)} 
        = 2\sqrt{2}|x-\xi|M^{-1}
        = \varepsilon |x-\xi|. 
    \end{equation*}
    If, on the other hand, \(x = \xi\), then the above inequality is trivially true as \(u(\xi,t_0)=0\).

For \(\lambda \geq 1\), consider the stretched hypersurface of revolution \(\Sigma_{u_\lambda}\), whose profile curve is the graph of \(u_{\lambda}\), for \(y\in [0,\lambda p]\). Using \eqref{eq:principal_curvatures}, the principal curvatures of \(\Sigma_{u_\lambda}\) scale as 
    \begin{align*}
        \kappa_1 = - \frac{u_{xx}(\frac{y}{\lambda})/\lambda^2}{\left(1 + |u_x(\frac{y}{\lambda})|^2/\lambda^2\right)^\frac{3}{2}}, &&
        \kappa_2 = \frac{1}{\left(1+|u_x(\frac{y}{\lambda})|^2/\lambda^2\right)^\frac{1}{2}u(\frac{y}{\lambda})}, &&
        \kappa_3 = -\frac{u_{x}}{\left(1 + |u_x(\frac{y}{\lambda})|^2/\lambda^2\right)^\frac{1}{2}y}.
    \end{align*}
    If \(y\in [\lambda p, \ \lambda \xi_2]\), the principal curvatures at points in the corresponding portion of \(\Sigma_{u_\lambda}\) are all strictly positive by assumption, so we have convexity in this region for any \(\lambda\). 

    If \(y\in[0,\lambda p]\), then the smoothness of \(\Sigma_u\) (in particular conditions \eqref{eq:crossing_P2_smoothness}) imply that there exists \(C\) such that 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:derivative_assumptions}
        \sup_{x\in [0, \ p]} \left(|u| + |u_x| + \frac{|u_x|}{|x|}+ |u_{xx}|\right) \leq C,
    \end{align}
    which yields the lower bounds
    \begin{align*}
        \kappa_1 \geq -\frac{C}{\lambda^2}, &&
        \kappa_2 \geq \frac{1}{(1+C^2)^\frac{1}{2}C}, &&
        \kappa_3 \geq -\frac{C}{\lambda^2}.
    \end{align*}
    For any \(C\), we can find \(\lambda\geq 1\) large enough that \(\kappa_1+\kappa_2+\kappa_3 > 0\), and so \(\Sigma_{u_\lambda}\) is mean convex.

\label{lem:hyperboloid_comparison}
    Let \(\{M_t\}_{t>0}\) be a solution of mean curvature flow in \(\mathbb{R}^4\). If for some \(2/3\leq \beta \leq 1\) and some \(\varepsilon \geq 0\) the initial hypersurface \(M_0\) satisfies
    \begin{align*}
        M_0 \subset \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in \mathbb{R}^4: (1-\beta)(x_1^2 + x_2^2) \geq y_1^2 + y_2^2 - \varepsilon^2\}
    \end{align*}
    then
    \begin{align*}
        M_t \subset \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in \mathbb{R}^4: (1-\beta)(x_1^2 + x_2^2) \geq y_1^2 + y_2^2 + \beta t - \varepsilon^2\}
    \end{align*}
    as long as the solution stays smooth. If in addition \(\varepsilon > 0\) and \(M_0 = \Sigma_u\) satisfies hypothesis (H2), then a singularity has to form at the latest time of \(\varepsilon^2/\beta\).

\label{prop:non_vanishing}
    Given \(\varepsilon ,\rho > 0\), \(\beta < 1\), \(p > \rho\), define \(g,h: \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\) by 
    \begin{align*}
        g(x) \coloneqq \sqrt{\rho^2 - (x-p)^2} 
        \quad \textnormal{and} \quad
        h(x) \coloneqq \sqrt{(1-\beta)x^2 + \varepsilon^2}.
    \end{align*} 
    Fix \(2/3\beta<1\) and suppose that \(M_0 = \Sigma_{u}\) satisfies hypothesis (H2) and \(u\) is such that \(g < u < h\). If \(\rho\) and \(\varepsilon\) are such that \(\rho^2 > 6\varepsilon^2/\beta\), then the mean curvature flow \(\{M_t\}_{t>0}\) does not vanish at the first singular time.

Take \(\beta\), \(\rho\), \(\varepsilon\) determined by Proposition \ref{prop:non_vanishing}, and define \(\varrho \coloneqq \sqrt{\rho^2-\varepsilon^2/\beta}\). Now let \(a_1,\ a_2\) be such that \(u(a_i,0) = \varrho/2\) for \(i=1,2\) and let \(b_1, \ b_2\) be such that \(u(b_i,0) = \varrho \) for \(i=1,2\). The mean convexity of the flow then implies that \(u(x,t)<\varrho/2\) for \(x\in [0,a_1)\cup (a_2,\xi_2(t)]\) and \(u(x,t)<\varrho\) for \(x\in [0,b_1)\cup (b_2,\xi_2(t)]\), whenever \(t<t_0\). We now place a sphere of radius \(r_1 = \varrho/4\) at \((b_1,0,0,0)\), and spheres of radii \(r_2 = \sqrt{6\varepsilon^2/\beta}\) at \((b_1,0,\varrho/2,0)\), \((b_2,0,\varrho/2,0)\) and \((a_2,0,0,0)\) which are all contained within \(\Sigma_u\). This can be done up to restricting \(\varepsilon\) so that \(\sqrt{6\varepsilon^2/\beta}<\varrho/2\), and up to stretching the profile curve as in Lemma \ref{lem:mean_convexity} (which preserves mean convexity and the structure of the initial profile curve). Possibly further restricting \(\varepsilon\) so that \(\varepsilon < \sqrt{(\varrho/4)^2 - 6\varepsilon^2/\beta}\), the barrier at \((b_1,0,0,0)\) ensures that \(u(b_1,t)> \varepsilon\) for \(t<t_0\), the barrier at \((a_2,0,0,0)\) ensures that \(\xi_2(t)>a_2\) and the barriers at \((b_i,0,\varrho/2,0)\) for \(i=1,2\) ensure that \(u(b_i,t)>\varrho/2\) for all \(t<t_0\). See Figure \ref{fig:singularity_origin} for an illustration.

    \begin{figure}[h]
        \centering

        \begin{subfigure}[h]{\textwidth}
            \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{singularity_origin_1}
            \caption{The profile curve with barriers at time \(t=0\)}
            \label{fig:singuarlity_origin_1} 
        \end{subfigure}

        \begin{subfigure}[h]{\textwidth}
            \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{singularity_origin_2}
            \caption{The profile curve with barriers at time \(t=t'\)}
            \label{fig:singularity_origin_2}
        \end{subfigure}

        \caption{Application of the Sturmian theorem}
        \label{fig:singularity_origin}
    \end{figure}

    Fix \(t'<t_0\) and define \(c_r(t) = \sqrt{r^2-2(t-t')}\), then \(c_r(t)\) evolves according to \eqref{eq:evolution_equation} and satisfies \(c_r(0) = \sqrt{r^2 + 2t'}\) and \(c_r(t')=r\). For any \(\varrho/2 \leq r \leq M \coloneqq \max u(\cdot,t')\), since \(u(a_i,t)<\varrho/2\) for \(i = 1,2\), it follows that \(u(\cdot,t)-c_r(t)\) will never have a zero at \(a_i\). On the other hand \(u(\cdot,0)-c_r(0)=0\) has at most two zeros for these values of \(r\) by construction, therefore we can apply the Sturmian theorem \ref{thm:sturmian_theorem} to conclude that \(u(\cdot,t) - c_r(t)\) has at most two zeros in \([a_1,a_2]\times[0,t']\). The problem is that \(u\) may take values below \(\varrho/2\) on \([a_1,b_1]\) and \([b_2,a_2]\), as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:singularity_origin_2}, so we cannot rule out the possibility of a local minimum forming in these regions. However, we chose barriers at \((b_i,0,\varrho/2,0)\) for \(i=1,2\) precisely to ensure that \(u(b_i,t)\geq \varrho/2\) for \(t\leq t'\). Moreover, the maximum value of \(u(\cdot,t)\) is greater than \(\varrho\) and there must therefore be a global maximum in \([b_1,b_2]\), since \(u(\cdot,t)\leq \varrho\) outside of this region. Therefore there must be at least two zeros of \(u(\cdot, t') - c_r(t')\) in \([b_1,b_2]\) for \(\varrho/2<r<M\). By our previous application of the Sturmian theorem, it follows that there must be exactly two zeros, which combined with the structure of the curve means that the only critical point of \(u(\cdot,t')\) can be a global maximum.

    It remains to show that \(u_x(\cdot,t')\) on \([0,b_1]\) and \(u_x(\cdot,t')<0\) on \([b_2,\xi_2(t')]\). We note that it is not possible to apply the Sturmian theorem in these regions, instead we use the strong maximum principle to rule out the existence of other critical points (except for the local minimum at \(x=0\)). Specifically, differentiating the evolution equation \eqref{eq:evolution_equation} with respect to \(x\), we find that \(v = u_x\) satisfies
    \begin{align}\label{eq:gradient_evolution}
        v_t = \frac{1}{(1+u_x^2)}v_{xx} + \frac{1}{x}v_x + \left(\frac{1}{u^2} - 2 \frac{(u_{xx})^2}{(1+u_x^2)^2} - \frac{1}{x^2}\right)v.
    \end{align}
    By assumption, \(v(\cdot,0) >0\) on \((0,b_1]\) and \(v(\cdot,0)<0\) on \((b_2,\xi_2(t)]\). If there exists a first time \(t^*<t'\) at which \(v(x,t^*)\) for some \(x\in (0,b_1]\) or \(x\in [b_2,\xi_2(t))\), and \(U\) a neighbourhood of \(x\) avoiding \(0\) and \(\xi_2(t)\), then the strong maximum principle applied to \eqref{eq:gradient_evolution} implies that \(v\equiv 0\) in \(U\times [0,t^*]\), which contradicts the structure of the profile curve at the initial time.

Recall that at the singular time our solution \(M_{t_0}\) contains a spherical barrier of radius \(\varrho = \sqrt{\rho^2 - 6\varepsilon^2/\beta}\). Therefore, there must exist some \(b\) such that \(u(b,t_0)>\varrho\). Take \(\eta\) to be the supremum of the \(Z_{t_0}\), as defined in Corollary \ref{cor:singularity_connected}, then \(u(\cdot,t_0)\) is continuous on \([\eta,b]\) and differentiable on \([\eta,b)\), so we can apply the mean value theorem to conclude that there exists some \(a\) and \(\delta\) such that \(u^*_x(a,t_0) > \delta\). By continuity of the flow up to the first singular time, it follows that there exists \(\varepsilon\) such that \(u_x(a,t)> \delta/2\) for all \(t\in [t_0-\varepsilon, t_0]\). By the structure of the profile curve up to the first singular time given by Proposition \ref{prop:singularity_at_origin}, we must also have \(u_x(x,t)>0\) for \((x,t)\in (0,a)\times [t_0-\varepsilon,t_0)\).

    Recall that the gradient \(v= u_x\) satisfies the evolution equation \eqref{eq:gradient_evolution} 
    \begin{align*}
        v_t = \frac{v_{xx}}{1+v^2} + \frac{v_x}{x} + \left(\frac{1}{u^2} - \frac{2 v_x^2}{(1+ v^2)^2} - \frac{1}{x^2}\right)v 
        \eqqcolon \mathcal{L} v.
    \end{align*}
    Now, for any \(0<\sigma <c\), set \(\varphi(x) = \alpha (x-\sigma)^2\). Using that \(\varphi_t = 0\), the expression
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{L} \varphi - \varphi_t
        &= \frac{\varphi_{xx}}{1+\varphi^2} + \frac{\varphi_x}{x} + \left(\frac{1}{u^2} - \frac{2\varphi_x^2}{(1+\varphi^2)^2} - \frac{1}{x^2}\right)\varphi \\
        &> \left(\frac{\varphi_{xx}}{1+\varphi^2} - \frac{2\varphi_x^2\varphi}{(1+\varphi^2)^2} \right) + \left(\frac{\varphi_x}{x} - \frac{\varphi}{x^2}\right) \\
        & > \frac{2\alpha}{1 + \alpha^2(x-\sigma)^4}\left(1 - 4\alpha^2\tau^4\right)
        + \frac{\alpha(x-\sigma)}{x}\left(1 - \frac{\sigma}{x}\right)
    \end{align*}
    is strictly positive provided \(\alpha < (2\tau^2)^{-1}\). 

    Up to further decreasing \(\alpha\) (which will not affect the positivity of the above expression) we can ensure that \(\varphi(x)< u_x(t_0-\varepsilon)\) for \(x\in [\sigma,\tau]\) and also that \(\varphi(\sigma) < u_x(\sigma,t)\) and \(\varphi(\tau)< u_x(\tau,t)\) for \(t_0-\varepsilon < t<t_0\). We can therefore apply the weak maximum principle to conclude that \(\varphi(x) < u_x(x,t)\) for \((x,t)\in (\sigma,\tau)\times(t_0-\varepsilon, t_0)\). It follows by integrating that
    \begin{align*}
        u(x,t)\geq \frac{\alpha}{3} (x-\sigma)^3
    \end{align*}
    on this domain. Moreover, this bound is strictly positive when we restrict to \(x\in [c,d]\subset [\sigma,\tau]\), and can be extended to \(t_0\), since it is independent of \(t\).

By the same argument as in the proof of Proposition \ref{prop:isolated_singularity} we know that there is some point \(b\) at which \(u_x(b,t_0)>0\). Since we know \(u_x(\cdot,t_0)\) is strictly positive away from \(x=0\), the evolution equation for the gradient \eqref{eq:gradient_evolution} is still valid up to the singular time, and in particular on \([a, b] \times(t_0-\varepsilon,t_0]\) for any \(a < b\). If \(u_x(x,t_0)=0\) for some \(x\in (a,b)\), then by the strong maximum principle we would have \(u_{x}(\cdot,t)\equiv 0\) back in time, which contradicts the structure of the profile curve given by Proposition \ref{prop:isolated_singularity}. Therefore, there is a neighbourhood of the origin on which \(u_x(\cdot,t_0) > 0\). Combined with the flatness estimate in Lemma \ref{lem:singularities_are_flat}, this implies that for all \(\varepsilon\) there exists \(\mu_\varepsilon\) such that 
    \begin{align*}
        0 \leq (u^*(x,t_0)-u^*(0,t_0))/x \leq \varepsilon
    \end{align*}
    for \(x<\mu_\varepsilon\), so the the derivative as \(x\to 0^+\) exists and is zero. By symmetry across the \(y_1\)-axis, the limit as \(x\to 0^-\) exists and is equal to zero. Thus, \(u_x(0,t_0) = 0.\) 

    It remains to show continuous differentiability near zero, that is that \(\lim_{x\to 0}u_x(x,t_0) = 0\). By the same reasoning as Proposition \ref{prop:graphicality}, this is equivalent to showing that \(\lim_{p\to 0}\tilde{\nu}(p)\cdot \mathbf{e}_3 = 1\), where \(\tilde{\nu}(p)\) is the normal to the profile curve at some \(p\in P_0\). Suppose for a contradiction that for all \(\delta > 0\) there exists a sequence \(p_k\to 0\) such that \(\tilde{\nu}(p_k) \cdot \mathbf{e}_3 < 1-\delta\). By Theorem 1.10 of \cite{Haslh17}, the limit of the parabolic rescaling \((p,t)\mapsto (s_k^{-1}(p-p_k), \ s_k^{-2}(t-t_0))\), where \(s_k=H(p_k,t_0)\), converges locally smoothly as \(k\to\infty\) to a limit flow with convex time slices. We show that the only possible limit flow is a shrinking \(\mathbb{R}^2\times \mathbb{S}^1\) with the Euclidean factor parallel to \(P_1\) and the spherical factor parallel to \(P_2\).  On this cylinder we have \(\tilde{\nu}\cdot \mathbf{e}_3 \equiv 1\) which contradicts the existence of the sequence \(p_k\to 0\) satisfying \(\tilde{\nu}(p_k,t_0)\cdot \mathbf{e}_3 < 1-\delta\). 

    \begin{figure}[h]
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width = 0.65\textwidth]{cylinder_containment}
        \caption{The solid cylinders \(\ell_k\times D_k\)}
        \label{fig:cylinder_containment}
    \end{figure}

    We now demonstrate why the limit flow must be a shrinking \(\mathbb{R}^2\times \mathbb{S}^1\). First note that, without loss of generality, each \(p_k\) can be written as \((z_k,0,u(z_k,t_0),0)\) for \(z_k\downarrow 0\). Moreover, by the monotonicity of the profile curve near the origin, for each \(k\in\{1,2,\dots\}\), \(M_{t_0}\) contains the solid cylinder \(\ell_k \times D_k\), where \(\ell_{k} = \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in P_1:z_k\leq x_1\leq z_1+ \varepsilon, \ x_2 = 0\}\) for \(\varepsilon>0\) small, and \(D_k\) is a disc of radius \(u(z_k,t_0)\) centred at \((z_k,0,0,0)\) and parallel to \(P_2\), see Figure \ref{fig:cylinder_containment}. By similar reasoning to the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:disc_containment}, the limit flow will either contain a solid cylinder of the form \(\ell\times D\), where \(\ell =\{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in P_1:x_1\geq 0, x_2 = 0\}\) and \(D\) is some disc in \(P_2\), or it will contain \(H = \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in \mathbb{R}^4:x_1\geq 0, \ x_2 = 0, y_2 \leq 0\}\). In the latter case, since \(H\) is a cone, the blow-down of the limit flow will still contain \(H\), but must also be a self-similar shrinker by Theorem 1.11 of \cite{Haslh17}. This forces the limit flow to be a plane, which contradicts that we have normalised the mean curvature to be one along the sequence \(p_k\) and so it cannot vanish identically in the limit. It must therefore follow that the limit flow must contain \(\ell \times D\), and moreover, the intersection of the limit flow with \(P_2\) is precisely \(\partial D\) (by definition of \(D_k\)), which gives the spherical factor. On the other hand, we now now that the disc \(\tilde{D}\) of radius \(\xi(t_0)\), centred at the origin and parallel to \(P_1\) will not escape to infinity under parabolic rescaling. In fact, by similar reasoning to the proof of Lemma \ref{lem:disc_containment}, it must converge to a copy of \(\mathbb{R}^2\) parallel to \(P_1\). This completes the proof.

\label{eg:profile_curve}
    Consider \(u\in C^0([0,p+1])\cap C^\infty([0,p+1))\) given by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:profile_curve}
        u(x) = 
        \begin{cases}
            \alpha x^2 + \mu & \text{ for } x\in [0,p-1] \\
            \psi(x) & \text{ for } x\in \left(p-1,\ p-\frac{1}{2}\right) \\
            \sqrt{1-(x-p)^2} & \text{ for } x\in \left[p-\frac{1}{2},\ p+1\right],
        \end{cases}
    \end{align}
    with \(\alpha,\ \mu\) small enough that \(u(p-1)<u(p)\), and \(\psi\) a smooth, strictly increasing interpolation function. The definition of \(u\) ensures that \(u_x>0\) for \(x\in (0,p)\) and \(u_x, \ u_{xx} <0\) for \(x\in (p,\xi_2)\) independent of its parameters; this structure is needed to apply Lemma \ref{lem:mean_convexity} and Proposition \ref{prop:singularity_at_origin}.

    \begin{figure}[h]
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width = 0.75\textwidth]{profile_curve}
        \caption{The profile curve \(u\) constructed in equation \eqref{eq:profile_curve}}
        \label{fig:profile_curve}
    \end{figure}

    By construction, the graph of \(u\) bounds the graph of \(g(x) = \sqrt{({1}/{2})^2 - (x-p)^2}\) from above, and this is independent of the choice of \(p,\ \alpha\) and \(\mu\). Now fix some \(2/3\leq \beta < 1\), and take any \(\varepsilon < \varepsilon_0\), where \(\varepsilon_0\) is determined by Proposition \ref{prop:singularity_at_origin}. We now adjust the parameters of \(u\) to ensure that the graph of \(h(x) = \sqrt{(1-\beta)x^2 + \varepsilon^2}\) lies above the graph of \(u\) for this choice of \(\beta\) and \(\varepsilon\). First pick \(p\) large enough that \(h(p-1) > 1\), then by the monotonicity of \(h\) and the fact that 1 is a global maximum for \(u\), 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:hyperbola_bound_1}
        u(x) \leq 1 < h(p-1) < h(x) \quad \textnormal{ for } p-1 < x \leq p.
    \end{align}
    Having made a choice of \(p\), pick \(\alpha\) and \(\mu\) small enough that \(\alpha(p-1)^2 + \mu < \varepsilon\), then 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:hyperbola_bound_2}
        u(x) \leq \alpha(p-1)^2 + \mu < \varepsilon = h(0) < h(x) \quad \textnormal{ for } 0\leq x\leq p-1.
    \end{align}
    Combining \eqref{eq:hyperbola_bound_1} and \eqref{eq:hyperbola_bound_2}, our choices of \(p,\mu\) and \(\alpha\) ensure that \(u(x) < h(x)\) for all \(0\leq x \leq p\), so we satisfy the assumptions of Proposition \ref{prop:non_vanishing}, which ensures that the flow is non-vanishing at the first singular time.

    Next, note that \(u\) satisfies the conditions of Lemma \ref{lem:mean_convexity}, so if \(\Sigma_u\) is not already mean convex, we can apply Lemma \ref{lem:mean_convexity} to find some \(\lambda>1\) such that the stretched hypersurface \(\Sigma_{u_\lambda}\) is mean convex. Moreover, if the graph of \(u\) lies below a hyperbola, then so does the graph of the rescaled function \(u_\lambda\). On the other hand, the graph of \(u_\lambda\) bounds the half circle of radius \(\frac{1}{2}\) and centre \((\lambda p, 0,0,0)\). Therefore our profile curve still satisfies the assumptions of Proposition \ref{prop:non_vanishing}, and additionally satisfies the mean convexity assumption of Proposition \ref{prop:singularity_at_origin}, so at the first singular time an isolated singularity forms at the origin.

\label{lem:u_xx_bound}
    Let \(M_0 = \Sigma_u\) be mean convex and satisfy hypothesis (H1) or (H2). For all \(0\leq t<t_0\) there exists some \(C>0\) such that
    \begin{align}\label{eq:u_xx_bound}
        \frac{u_{xx}}{1+u_x^2} \leq \frac{C}{1+C}\left(\frac{1}{u} - \frac{u_x}{x}\right).
    \end{align}

For any mean convex mean curvature flow, it was shown by Huisken and Sinestrari that there exists uniform \(C=C(M_0)>0\) such that \(|A|^2/H^2< C^2\) on \(M_t\) for \(0\leq t< t_0\), where \(A\) denotes the second fundamental form \cite{Huisk99}. Using the expression for the principal curvatures given by \eqref{eq:principal_curvatures}, one finds that 
    \begin{align*}
        |A|^2 = \frac{u_{xx}^2}{(1+u_x^2)^3} + \frac{1}{u^2(1+u_x^2)} + \frac{u_x^2}{x^2(1+u_x^2)},
    \end{align*}
    and so
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{u_{xx}^2}{(1+u_x^2)^3} 
        \leq |A|^2 
        \leq C^2 H^2
        = C^2 \left(-\frac{u_{xx}}{(1+u_x^2)^{\frac{3}{2}}} + \frac{1}{u(1+u_x^2)^{\frac{1}{2}}} -\frac{u_x}{x(1+u_x^2)^\frac{1}{2}} \right)^2.
    \end{align*}
    Multiplying by \(1+u_x^2\) and taking a square root (using that \(H>0\) by mean convexity) we obtain
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{u_{xx}}{1+u_x^2} 
        \leq C \left(- \frac{u_{xx}}{1+u_x^2} + \frac{1}{u} - \frac{u_x}{x}\right) 
    \end{align*}
    Rearranging gives the result.

\label{lem:mean_convex_approximation}
    Let \(\Sigma_{u^*}\) be as before. Then \(\Sigma_{u^*}\) admits an approximation by smooth, mean convex hypersurfaces \(\Sigma_{u^j}\) represented by functions \(u^j\in C^0([\delta_02^{-(j+1)},\xi_2(t_0)])\cap C^\infty((\delta_0 2^{-(j+1)},\xi_2(t_0)))\) such that \(u^{j+1}\leq u^j \leq u^*\)for sufficiently small \(\delta_0>0\). Specifically, the approximation satisfies
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:approximation_property}
        u^j(x)
        \begin{cases}
            \leq {u}^*(x) & \textnormal{ if } {2^{-(j+1)}}\delta_0\leq x \leq 2^{-j}\delta_0 \\[2pt]
            = {u}^*(x) & \textnormal{ if } x > {2^{-j}}{\delta_0}. 
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
    Moreover, \(u_x^j>0\) whenever \(u^j \neq u^*\).

By Lemma \ref{lem:differentiability}, \(u^*\) is continuously differentiable and monotonic increasing in a neighbourhood of \(0\) and \(u^*(0) = u_x^*(0) = 0\). Therefore given \(\varepsilon>0\) to be determined later, there exists \(\delta_0\) such that
    \begin{align}\label{eq:epsilon_bounds}
        0 \leq u_x^*(x) < \varepsilon 
        && \textnormal{and} && 
        u^*(x) < \varepsilon x
    \end{align}
    for \(x\in[0,\delta_0]\). 

    To construct our approximation, we interpolate between \(u^*\) and an appropriately chosen function \(p^j\), defined on \([2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0,2^{-j}\delta_0]\). We require that \(\Sigma_{p^j}\) is smooth and mean convex, and also that \(p^j(2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0) = 0\) and \(p^j\leq m_j\) where \(m_j\) is the minimum value achieved by \(u^*\) on \([2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0,2^{-j}\delta_0]\). Due to the monotonicity of \(u^*\), this value is always achieved at \(2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0\), and so \(m^j \leq \varepsilon 2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0\). We claim that 
    \begin{align*}
        p^j(x) = m_j(2^{j+1}\delta_0^{-1}x -1)^{1/2}
    \end{align*}
    has these properties; all except mean convexity are immediate. To show mean convexity, we calculate
    \begin{align*}
        p_x^j = m_j 2^j\delta_0^{-1}(2^{j+1}\delta_0^{-1}x-1)^{-1/2} 
        && \textnormal{and} &&
        p_{xx}^j(x) = -m_j (2^j\delta_0^{-1})^2(2^{j+1}\delta_0^{-1}x-1)^{-3/2}.
    \end{align*} 
    Since \(p_{xx}^j\) is negative, it is enough to show that \(\frac{1}{p^j} - \frac{p_x^j}{x} > 0\). Using that \(m_j\leq \varepsilon 2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0\) and \(x\geq 2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0\), we obtain the bounds
    \begin{align*}
        p^j(x) \leq \varepsilon 2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0(2^{j+1}\delta_0^{-1}x - 1)^{1/2} 
        && \textnormal{and} &&
        \frac{p^j_x(x)}{x} \leq \varepsilon 2^j\delta_0^{-1}(2^{j+1}\delta_0^{-1}x-1)^{-1/2}.
    \end{align*}
    It follows that
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{1}{p^j(x)} - \frac{p_x^j(x)}{x} 
        > 2^j\delta_0^{-1}(2\varepsilon^{-1}- \varepsilon)(2^{j+1}\delta_0^{-1}x-1)^{-1/2} >0,
    \end{align*}
    provided \(\varepsilon\) is chosen small enough that \(2\varepsilon^{-1}-\varepsilon>0\).

    \begin{figure}[h]
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width = 0.85\textwidth]{interpolation}
        \caption{The interpolation function \(u^j\)}
        \label{fig:interpolation}
    \end{figure}

    Now consider any smooth, monotonic increasing function \(\varphi^j\) on \([2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0, \ 2^{-j}\delta_0]\) such that \(\varphi^j \equiv 0\) on \([2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0, \ 5\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0]\), \(\varphi^j \equiv 1\) on \([7\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0, \ 2^{-j}\delta_0]\), and \(0\leq \varphi^j \leq 1\), \(\varphi_x^j>0\) on \((5\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0, \ 7\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0)\). Additionally, \(\varphi^j\) should satisfy 
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{x\in[5\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0, 7\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0]} \varphi_x^j(x) \leq C_1 2^{j+3}\delta_0^{-1}
        &&
        \sup_{x\in[5\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0, 7\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0]} |\varphi_{xx}^j(x)| \leq C_2 (2^{j+3}\delta_0^{-1})^2
    \end{align*}
    for some \(C_1, C_2 > 0\) independent of \(j\). there are many standard constructions of such a function. Define \(u^j\coloneqq \varphi^ju^* + (1-\varphi^j)p^j\) on \(x\in [2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0,2^{-j}\delta_0]\). Then \(u^j = p^j\) on \([2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0,\ 5\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0]\) and \(u^j = u^*\) on \([7\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0,\ 2^{-j}\delta_0]\). Thus we can set \(u^j = u^*\) on \([2^{-j}\delta_0, \ \xi_2]\) to ensure that \(u^j\) satisfies \eqref{eq:approximation_property}. See Figure \ref{fig:interpolation} for an illustration.

    It remains to show the mean convexity of \(u^j\) in the region \((5\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0,\ 7\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0)\). It suffices to show that 
    \begin{align*}
        -\frac{u_{xx}^j}{1+(u_x^j)^2} + \frac{1}{u^j} - \frac{u_x^j}{x} > 0.
    \end{align*}
    We start by finding lower bounds for each term individually. First, \(u^j \leq u^*\) by construction, so 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:second_term_bound}
        \frac{1}{u^j} \geq \frac{1}{u^*}.
    \end{align}
    Next, note that \(p_x^j\) achieves a maximum value of \(\varepsilon\) at \(5\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0\), so
    \begin{align*}
        u_x^j = \varphi_x^j(u^*-p^j) + \varphi^ju_x^* + (1-\varphi^j)p_x^j
        \leq \varphi_x^ju^* + u_x^* + p_x^j \leq \varepsilon(8C_1 + 2)
    \end{align*} 
    where we have also used that \(u_x^*(x)\leq \varepsilon\) and \(u^*(x)\leq u^*(2^{-j}\delta_0)\leq \varepsilon 2^{-j}\delta_0\) (see \eqref{eq:epsilon_bounds}). Combined with the fact that \(x> 2^{-(j+1)}\delta_0\), it follows that
    \begin{align}\label{eq:third_term_bound}
        -\frac{u_x^j}{x}\geq -\varepsilon2^j\delta_0^{-1}C_1'
    \end{align}
    for \(C_1' = (8C_1+2)\). Finally, using that \(|p_{xx}^j|\) achieves a maximum value of \(4\varepsilon2^j\delta_0^{-1}\) at \(5\cdot 2^{-(j+3)}\delta_0\) as well as the upper bounds on \(u^*\) and \(u^*_x\) given by \eqref{eq:epsilon_bounds}, we have
    \begin{align*}
        u_{xx}^j 
        &= \varphi_{xx}^j(u^*-p^j) + 2\varphi_x^j(u_x^* - p_x^j) + \varphi^ju_{xx}^* + (1-\varphi^j)p_{xx}^j \\
        &\leq |\varphi_{xx}^j|(|u^*| + |p^j|) + 2|\varphi_{x}|(|u_x^*| + |p^j_x|) + (1-\varphi^j)|p_{xx}^j| + \varphi^ju_{xx}^* \\
        &\leq \varepsilon2^j\delta_0^{-1}(128 C_2 + 32C_1 + 4) + \varphi^ju_{xx}^*.
    \end{align*}
    It follows from Lemma \ref{lem:u_xx_bound} (which can be extended up to the singular time away from the singular set) that there exists \(C>0\) such that
    \begin{align*}
        u_{xx}^* \leq C(1+C)^{-1}(1+\varepsilon^2)\frac{1}{u^*},
    \end{align*}
    where we have also used that \(u_x^*\geq 0\) in the region we are considering. Therefore, 
    \begin{align*}
        u_{xx}^j \leq \varepsilon2^j\delta_0^{-1}C_2' + C(1+C)^{-1}(1+\varepsilon^2) \frac{1}{u^*},
    \end{align*}
    where \(C_2' = 128C_2 + 32 C_1 + 4\). The right hand side of this inequality is positive, so 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:first_term_bound}
        -\frac{u_{xx}^j}{1 + (u_x^j)^2} 
        \geq -\varepsilon2^j\delta_0^{-1}C_2' - C(1+C)^{-1}(1+\varepsilon^2)\frac{1}{u^*}.
    \end{align}
    Combining inequalities \eqref{eq:second_term_bound}, \eqref{eq:third_term_bound}, \eqref{eq:first_term_bound} and using that \(1/u^* \geq \varepsilon^{-1}2^j\delta_0^{-1}\), we have
    \begin{align*}
        -\frac{u_{xx}^j}{1+(u_x^j)^2} + \frac{1}{u^j} - \frac{u_x^j}{x} 
        &\geq \frac{1}{u^*}(1-C(1+C)^{-1}(1+\varepsilon^2)) - \varepsilon 2^j\delta_0^{-1}(C_1' + C_2')\\
        &\geq 2^j\delta_0^{-1}(\varepsilon^{-1}(1+C)^{-1} -\varepsilon(C(1+C)^{-1}+ C_1' + C_2')),
    \end{align*}
    which is positive for \(\varepsilon\) small enough. Note that our choice of \(\varepsilon\) depends only on \(C\), \(C_1\), \(C_2'\) and in particular can be chosen independently of \(j\).

\label{prop:evans_spruck}
    For \(s_1 < s_2\), \(\rho>0\) and \(q\in \mathbb{R}^n\), set 
    \begin{align*}
        \Omega = B_\rho(q)\times (s_1,s_2).
    \end{align*}
    Suppose that \(v\in C^3(\Omega)\) solves the full graph equation in \(\Omega\) with \(M=\sup_\Omega|v|<\infty\). For any \(\varepsilon>0\), there is a constant \(C = C(M,\varepsilon,n)\) such that
    \begin{align*}
        |\nabla v|\leq C 
        \quad \textnormal{ on } \quad  
        \Omega_{\varepsilon} = B_{\rho-\varepsilon}(q)\times(s_1+\varepsilon^2,s_2).
    \end{align*}

\label{prop:evans_spruck_schauder}
    Let \(\Omega\) be as before, and suppose that \(v\in C^3(\Omega)\) solves the full graph equation in \(\Omega\) with \(M=\sup_\Omega |v|<\infty\) and \(N = \sup_\Omega|\nabla v|<\infty\). Then for \(\varepsilon>0\) and non-negative \(\ell \in \mathbb{N}\), there exists \(C' = C'(M,N,\varepsilon,n,\ell)\) such that 
    \begin{align*}
        \|v\|_{C^\ell(\bar{\Omega}_\varepsilon)}\leq C'.
    \end{align*}

It follows from Lemma \ref{lem:differentiability} that \(u^*_x >0\) in a neighbourhood of the origin and from \eqref{eq:crossing_P2_smoothness} that \(u^*_x<0\) in a neighbourhood of \(\xi_2\), so the approximation \(u^j\) is \(\alpha\)-invertible for some \(\alpha\) uniform in \(j\). It then follows from Lemma \ref{lem:alpha_invertible} that there exist \(\alpha_0, \ t_0>0\) such that all the \(u^j\)s are \(\alpha_0\)-invertible for \(0<t< t_0\). Recall that we want to split our analysis into two regions, near to and away from the origin. Near to the origin, we argue using the Evans-Spruck gradient estimates, and away from the origin we argue using pseudolocality. In order to patch these two arguments together, we need to ensure that the domains on which we are applying these two arguments overlap on some section of the profile curve.

    To this end, let \(p\) be such that \(u^*(p) = \alpha_0\). Then for small enough \(\varepsilon_0\), we can place a sphere of radius \(\varepsilon_0\) in the interior of \(\Sigma_u\) and this containment holds with \(\Sigma_{u^j}\) replacing \(\Sigma_{u^*}\), up to discarding finitely many \(\Sigma_{u^j}\)s. Then by the avoidance principle, the sphere of radius \(\varepsilon(t) = \sqrt{\varepsilon_0^2-6t}\) remains in the interior of \(F_t(\Sigma_{u^j})\) for \(0\leq t\leq \min\{t_0,\varepsilon_0^2/6\}\). Let \(p_{\varepsilon_0} = p - \varepsilon_0/2\), then \(\varepsilon(t)\geq {\varepsilon_0}/\sqrt{2}\) for \(0\leq t \leq \min\{t_0,\varepsilon_0^2/12\}\eqqcolon T\), which implies that \(\xi^j_1(t) < p_{\varepsilon_0}\), where \(\xi^j_1(t)\) is the left end-point of \(u^j\) at time \(t\). On the other hand, it follows from mean convexity of the flow that for \(x\in (p_{\varepsilon_0},p)\) and any \(j\geq 1\), we must have \(u^j(x,t)<\alpha_0\) for \(t< T\). Let
    \begin{align*}
        X = \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in P_0:0\leq x_1\leq p, \ 0\leq y_1 < \alpha_0\} 
        && \textnormal{and} &&
        Y = \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in P_0: x_1 \geq p_{\varepsilon_0}\}
    \end{align*}
    then by the above reasoning some portion of the graph of \(u^j(x,t)\) must lie within \(X\cap Y\). Due to this overlap, to obtain a uniform bound on all derivatives of the second fundamental form of \(F_t(\Sigma_{u^j})\), it suffices to find such a bound for points on the flow lying in \(X\) and \(Y\) separately, and then invoke the \(SO(2)\times SO(2)\)-invariance of \(F_t(\Sigma_{u^j})\).

    \smallskip

    \noindent{\bf Case 1: Close to the origin.} We start by showing that close to the origin portions of \(F_t(\Sigma_{u^j})\) satisfy the full graph equation over a domains independent of \(t\) and \(j\). This relies on a quantitative lower bound on the distance between the evolving surface and the origin. Specifically, the first part of Lemma \ref{lem:hyperboloid_comparison} applied with \(\varepsilon=0\) tells us that if for some \(2/3\leq \beta < 1\), 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:Sigma_u^j_containment}
        \Sigma_{u^j} \subset \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in \mathbb{R}^4:(1-\beta)(x_1^2 + x_2^2) \geq y_1^2 + y_2^2\}
    \end{align}
    then the level set flow satisfies
    \begin{align}\label{eq:level_set_flow_containment}
        F_{t}(\Sigma_{u^j}) \subset \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in \mathbb{R}^4: (1-\beta)(x_1^2 + x_2^2) \geq y_1^2 + y_2^2 + \beta t\}
    \end{align}
    for all \(t\), so long as the solution stays smooth for this time. Since \(\textnormal{int}(\Sigma_{u^j})\subset \textnormal{int}(\Sigma_{u^*})\), the parameter \(\beta\) can be chosen to depend only on \(\Sigma_{u^*}\), so the lower bound is uniform in \(j\). If necessary, the containment \eqref{eq:Sigma_u^j_containment} can be achieved by further stretching the profile curve constructed in Example \ref{eg:profile_curve} along the \(x_1\)-axis.

    \begin{figure}[h]
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width = 0.8\textwidth]{covering}
        \caption{Regions of interest for Evans-Spruck and pseudolocality} 
        \label{fig:covering}
    \end{figure}

    By construction, each \(u^j(\cdot,t)\big|_{[0,p]}\) has a well defined inverse \(v^j:[0,\alpha_0)\to [0,p]\) for  \(t\in [\delta,T)\), where \(p\) is such that \(u^*(p) = \alpha_0\). Moreover, expression \eqref{eq:level_set_flow_containment} implies that \(v^j(y,t)\geq \beta t\) for \(y\in[0,\alpha_0)\) and \(t \in [\delta,T)\), which ensures that 
    \begin{align*}
        |x_2|<\beta\delta \leq \min \{v^j(y,t):y\in [0, \alpha_0), \ t\in [\delta, T), \ j\geq 1\}.
    \end{align*}
    Further restricting \(T\) if necessary so that \(\beta T< \alpha_0\), the above inequality tells us that a portion of \(F_t(\Sigma_{u^j})\) can be written as a graph \(\tilde{v}_0^j\) over the 3-ball \(B_{\beta\delta}(0)\cap \{x_1=0\}\) with \(M_0 \coloneqq \sup |\tilde{v}_0^j| \leq p\). Note in particular that the domain and \(M_0\) are independent of both \(t\) and \(j\). To cover all of \(F_t(\Sigma_{u^j})\cap X\), we can repeat this process to define graphs \(\{\tilde{v}_\ell^j\}_{\ell=1}^N\) on balls of radius \(\beta\delta\) centred at points \((0,0,\ell\beta\delta,0)\), and will also require one final ball centred at \((0,0,\alpha-\beta\delta,0)\) to cover any remaining gap. More formally, set \(N = \lfloor \frac{\alpha_0}{\beta\delta}\rfloor\) and for \(\ell\in \{0,\dots,N\}\) define
    \begin{align*}
        \tilde{v}^j_\ell:\ &B_{\beta\delta}(z_\ell)\cap \{x_1=0\}\times [\delta,T) \to [0,p]\\
        &(x_2,y_1,y_2,t)\mapsto\sqrt{\left(v^j\left(\sqrt{(y_1-\ell\beta\delta)^2 + y_2^2},\ t\right)\right)^2 - x_2^2}
    \end{align*}
    where \(z_\ell = (0,0,\ell\beta\delta,0)\). Then define
    \begin{align*}
        \tilde{v}^j_{N+1}:\ &B_{\beta\delta}(z_{N+1})\cap \{x_1=0\} \times [\delta, T) \to [0,p] \\
        &(x_2,y_1,y_2,t) \mapsto \sqrt{\left(v^j\left(\sqrt{(y_1-(\alpha_0-\beta\delta))^2 + y_2^2},\ t\right)\right)^2 - x_2^2}
    \end{align*}
    where \(z_{N+1} = (0,0,\alpha_0-\beta\delta,0)\). See Figure \ref{fig:covering} for an illustration.

    Since each \(\tilde{v}^j_\ell\) is smooth and solves the full graph equation \eqref{eq:full_graph_equation} in \(B_{\beta\delta}(z_k)\cap\{x_1=0\}\times[\delta,T)\), applying the Evans-Spruck estimate from Proposition \ref{prop:evans_spruck} with \(M_\ell \coloneqq \sup|\tilde{v}^j_k| \leq p\), then upgrading the regularity using Propostion \ref{prop:evans_spruck_schauder}, it follows that for any \(\varepsilon>0\), the first \(k\) derivatives of \(\tilde{v}^j_\ell\) are uniformly bounded on \(B_{\beta\delta-\varepsilon}(z_\ell)\cap\{x_1=0\}\times(\delta+\varepsilon^2,T)\), which implies uniform bounds on the first \(k-2\) derivatives of the second fundamental forms of these graphs, each of which cover a small portion of \(F_t(\Sigma_{u^j})\). Note that the balls over which these graphs are defined cover all of \(\{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in P_0: x_1 = 0, \ \varepsilon - \beta \delta \leq y_1 \leq \alpha - \varepsilon\}\), and \(\tilde{v}^j_\ell(0,y,0,t) = v^j(y,t)\) for \(y\in B_{\beta\delta-\varepsilon}(z_\ell)\cap P_0\). In particular this implies that the first \(k-2\) derivatives of the second fundamental form of \(F_t(\Sigma_{u^j})\) are uniformly bounded at points in \(X\).

    \smallskip

    \noindent{\bf Case 2: Away from the origin.} We now prove uniform derivative bounds away from the origin by means of a pseudolocality argument. First, let \(q\) be such that \({u}^*(q)=\varepsilon_0/2\). By property \eqref{eq:approximation_property} of the approximation, we know that \(u^j(x) = {u}^*(x)\) for \(x\geq \delta_0 2^{-j}\), where \(\delta_0\) is a fixed constant depending only on \({u}^*\). Thus, up to discarding finitely many \(u^j\), it holds that \(u^j(x) = {u}^*(x)\) for all \(x\geq q\). Since \(\Sigma_{u^*}\) is smooth away from the origin, we can write \(\Sigma_{u^j} \cap \{(\bar{x},\bar{y})\in P_0: x_1\geq q\}\) as finitely many smooth graphs \(\tilde{u}_\ell\) over finitely many domains \(\Omega_\ell\) so that \(\|\tilde{u}_\ell\|_{C^4}(\Omega_\ell)\leq M\) for \(\ell \in \{1,\dots,L\}\). Crucially, these graphs are defined independently of \(j\).

    Now, given \(\beta_0\) as in Proposition \ref{prop:pseudolocality}, choose \(r\) small enough so that \(M\leq \beta_0r^{-3}\). Then it is possible to cover \(\Sigma_{u^j}\cap Y\) with 2-balls \(\{B_r(z_k)\cap P_0\}_{k=1}^N\) such that \(z_1 = (p_{\varepsilon_0},0,u^*(p_{\varepsilon_0}),0)\), \(z_N = (p_{\varepsilon_0},0,-u^*(p_{\varepsilon_0}),0)\) and \(P_0 \cap \bigcup_{k=1}^N B_r(z_k)\) contains a tubular neighbourhood of radius \(r/2\) of \(\Sigma_{u^j}\cap Y\), which we denote by \(V\). Possibly shrinking \(r\), we can ensure that the collection \(\{B_{4r}(z_i) \cap P_0\}_{k=1}^N\) consisting of 2-balls of radius \(4r\) still covers \(\Sigma_{u^j}\cap Y\) without touching two disconnected segments of the hypersurface (this is only a problem near the origin). See Figure \ref{fig:covering} for an illustration. Each \(\Sigma_{u^j}\cap B_{4r}(z_k)\) is then a restriction of the graph \(\tilde{u}_\ell\) to some \(\bar{\Omega}_{k,\ell} \subset \bar{\Omega}_\ell\), which satisfies \(\|\tilde{u}_\ell\big|_{\bar{\Omega}_{k,\ell}}\|_{C^4}\leq M\leq \beta_0/r^4\), for any \(k\in\{1,\dots,N\}\), up to slightly modifying the covering or the domains \(\bar{\Omega}_\ell\). 

    We can now apply Proposition \ref{prop:pseudolocality} to conclude that there exists \(C\) independent of \(j\) such that \(|A^j(w,t)|\leq Cr^{-1}\) for all \(t\leq \min\{r^2\beta_0,T\}\) and \(w\in F_t(\Sigma_{u^j})\cap B_r(z_k)\), for \(T>0\) determined in Case 1. To conclude a uniform second fundamental form bound in the cylindrical region \(Y\), we need to show that there exists a time \(t^*>0\) such that any point on \(F_t(\Sigma_{u^j})\cap Y\) lies in one of the 2-balls \(P_0\cap B_r(z_k)\) and hence in \(B_r(z_k)\). Symbolically, we require
    \begin{align*}
        Y \cap F_t(\Sigma_{u^j}) \subset P_0\cap {\textstyle\bigcup_{k=1}^N} B_r(z_k),
    \end{align*}
    for \(t<t^*\). To prove this, note that for any \(x\in P_0\) we have \(\operatorname{dist}(x,\Sigma_{u^j})=\operatorname{dist}(x,\Sigma_{u^j}\cap P_0)\), by the symmetry of \(\Sigma_{u^j}\), and by the avoidance principle, \(\operatorname{dist}(w,\Sigma_{u^j})\leq \sqrt{6t}\) for any \(w\in P_0\cap F_t(\Sigma_{u^j})\). If \(t^*\) is chosen so that \(\sqrt{6t^*}\leq r/2\), then \(\operatorname{dist}(w,\Sigma_{u^j}\cap P_0) \leq r/2\), so \(w\) belongs to the tubular neighbourhood \(V \subset \bigcup_{k=1}^N B_r(z_k)\) that we constructed earlier. Therefore the result holds for \(t\leq\min\{r^2\beta_0,\ r^2/24,\ T\}\). A uniform bound on higher derivatives of the second fundamental form can then be found using \cite[Proposition 3.22]{Ecker04}.

\label{rem:higher_dimensions}
    Very few modifications are required to extend the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:main_theorem} from \(SO(2)\times SO(2)\) invariant flow in \(\mathbb{R}^4\) to \(SO(p)\times SO(q)\)-invariant flow in \(\mathbb{R}^{p+q}\). The most substantial modification is required in Section \ref{sec:singularity_formation}, where White's classification result allows for larger number of potential tangent flows that need to be excluded. However, the exact statement of Lemma \ref{lem:disc_containment} still holds in higher dimensions, with the only change being that the \(\ell\) becomes a \((p-1)\)-dimensional plane in \(P_1\), \(H_1^-\) and \(H_1^+\) both become \(p\)-dimensional half-planes in \(P_1\), and \(H_2^-\) becomes a \(q\)-dimensional plane in \(P_2\). This allows the additional tangent flows to be ruled out in bulk using an almost identical argument to the present one. Similar modifications will need to be made when considering the limit flow in Lemma \ref{lem:differentiability}.

    Aside from this, many of the standard results for mean curvature flow that are used in this paper involve constants that depend implicitly on the dimension, but not in a way that fundamentally changes any arguments. In particular, the rates of shrinking spheres and cylinders will need to be adjusted appropriately. The one notable adjustment on this front relates to the admissible range of \(\beta\) for the hyperboloid comparison principle (Lemma \ref{lem:hyperboloid_comparison}) which is not standard in the literature. In the general case, we require \(0\leq \beta \leq 1\) when \(p\leq q-1\), and \(2(p-q+1)/(2p-1)\leq \beta \leq 1\) when \(p>q-1\).
